  "source_dataset": "MIMIC_IV_NOTE",
  "text": "Allergies:\nPenicillins\n\nChief Complaint:\nSeizures\n\nHistory of Present Illness:\nMr. ___ is a ___ year old left handed man with history of\nepilepsy secondary to developmental brain malformations with\nrecurrent focal motor seizures who presents with increased\nseizure frequency.  Patient's epilepsy is in the setting of left\nmesial frontal subcortical gray matter heterotopia.  He has \nfocal\nmotor seizures which involve rhythmic shaking of RUE/RLE with\npreserved consciousness and occasional head/eye deviation to the\nright.  He is followed in clinic by Dr. ___ and was\nlast seen ___ at which time no medication changes were made. \n\nHis current AEDs are Keppra XR 300mg qd, Zonegran 300mg qhs and\nlorazepam 1mg prn seizure.  On average, he has seizures quite\ninfrequently.  Most recent was in ___ of this year, and before,\nin ___ and ___.\n\nYesterday, Mr. ___ \"didn't feel quite right\" when he woke up, \n\nmaybe a bit drowsy.  He had a very active day of furniture\nshopping with his parents for his new apt.  When he was in a\nstore, his right leg seemed \"off,\" so he decided to go to the\nbathroom to sit down.  Per dad, he seemed to be dragging his\nright leg.  (He has a baseline mild R sided hemiparesis, but it\nis usually only evident when he is over exerting himself.)  Pt\nfelt his right leg had a mild tremor and a funny sensation \n(maybe\nfelt rubbery), as if something was trying to break through.  \nThis\nresolved after ~15 seconds.  He took an ativan just in case \n(this\nwas at 3pm).  He was doing well until 7:30pm when he was walking\ndown the stairs and lost his balance.  Per mom, his right leg\ngave away, lifted up and shook for ___ seconds.  He took\nanother ativan and went to sleep at around 9pm. \n\nWhen Mr. ___ up this morning, he was at his baseline. \nAt 9am, he was sitting at breakfast table at ~9am and felt his\nRLE tensed up, lifted and started shaking and then his RUE did \nas\nwell.  This lasted ___ seconds and he took an ativan.  He \nspoke\nwith Dr. ___ recommended increasing Zonegran by\nadding an extra 100mg in the morning, which he took.  He went to\nhis PCP to check basic labs/UA to assess for metabolic or\ninfectious disturbance that could potentially lower seizure\nthreshold and this was unremarkable.  He had 1 more seizure in\nthe car on the way to the PCP ___ 12:45 and went to the ED.  In\ntriage, had another, this time with RUE/RLE shaking and head\nversion to the right.  So, total of 5 seizures since yesterday. \nHe took a total of 3mg of ativan since yesterday.  No loss of\nawareness with any of the events.\n\nIn regards to seizure triggers: Pt has not missed any doses of\nAEDs.  He sleeps an average of ___ hours per night.  He has not\nconsumed EtOH since having seizures, no illicits.  He denies\ninfectious symptoms (fevers/chills, cough, rhinorrhea, dysuria,\ndiarrhea).  He has had some stress with moving into his new\napartment/buying furniture.    Of note, he lost 27 lbs recently. \n\nHe has NOT tried any unusal diets or clenses.  He eats healthy\nmeals with protein/fruits/vegetables and exercise regularly.\n\nEpilepsy history:  Pt's first seizure was in ___. He was\nborn two weeks post-term and an antenatal ultrasound showed\nagenesis of the corpus callosum.  He was found to have a mild\nright sided hemparesis at age ___, was evaluatd at ___, and had\nimaging that confirmed the presence of a lesion. He did have \nmild\nlearning disabilities, specfically in speech and language,\nrequiring tutors. He is doing well and graduated college at\n___ recently.   In regards to other epilepsy risk\nfactors, he had no birth injury, no CNS infections, no history \nof\nhead trauma with loss of consciousness, brain surgery/tumors, no\nfamily hx of heterotropia or other neurological disorders.   In\n___, he had his first focal motor seizure which involved\nrhythmic jerking of the right arm and leg lasting ~1 minute, no\nloss of consciousness or awareness.  He was taken to ___ where \nhe\nhad 3 more similar episodes, some with eye deviation to the \nright\nand possibly difficulty with expressive language.  His EEG at\n___ showed left temporalslowing during\ndrowsiness. MRI brain showed corpus callosum agenesis, left\nmesial frontal subcortical gray matter heterotopia. \nHe was started on Keppra 750 mg BID initially.  He continued to\nhave occasional break through seizures and AEDs were gradually\nuptitrated.  Currently, he is on Keppra and Zonisamide as above\nas well as ativan prn seizure. \n\nOn neuro ROS, the pt denies headache, loss of vision, blurred\nvision, diplopia, dysarthria, dysphagia, lightheadedness,\nvertigo, tinnitus or hearing difficulty. Denies difficulties\nproducing or comprehending speech.  Denies focal numbness,\nparasthesiae. No bowel or bladder incontinence or retention.\n \nOn general review of systems, the pt denies recent fever or\nchills.  No night sweats or recent weight loss or gain. Denies\ncough, shortness of breath.  Denies chest pain or tightness,\npalpitations.  Denies nausea, vomiting, diarrhea, constipation \nor\nabdominal pain.  No recent change in bowel or bladder habits.  \nNo\ndysuria.  Denies arthralgias or myalgias. Denies rash.\n\nBrief Hospital Course:\n___ was admitted to the epilepsy service where he remained \nseizure free for >48h and was subsequently discharged. On the \nmorning of his admission, he was asked to take an additional \n100mg of ZON to help treat his burst of seizure activity which \nwas caused by an as yet unknown trigger. He had been compliant \nwith his medications, had been in good health lately, and all \nscreening metabolic labs and his chest X-ray performed in the ED \nwere unremarkable. We subsequently increased his ZON to 400mg \ndaily which he tolerated well without any immediate side \neffects. He was monitored on EEG which showed no electrographic \nseizures. He had no pushbuttons either. \n\nWe discussed his case with Dr. ___ primary \nepileptologist. The decision was made to have him be discharged \non this higher dose of medication and he will return sometime in \nthe future for a possible admission to the EMU for medication \ntitration and event characterization. He will follow up with Dr. \n___.\n\nDischarge Diagnosis:\nSymptomatic epilepsy \nCortical dysplasia",
